<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- Sandbox off for local debug ONLY. With ad-hoc signing and no
	     DEVELOPMENT_TEAM there is no keychain-access-group to grant, so
	     SecItemAdd fails with -34018 ("a required entitlement isn't
	     present") and every native login dies at the moment it tries to
	     store the API key. Release keeps the sandbox. The real fix is a
	     signing team plus keychain-access-groups in both entitlements;
	     until that account exists, debug cannot reach the keychain at all.
	     Consequence to keep in mind: debug no longer mirrors release, so a
	     genuine entitlement problem will not surface until a release build.

	     Verified 2026-09-11 by compiling and running the app, reading the
	     system's own `secd` logs (not just the Flutter exception): the
	     theory that flutter_secure_storage's macOS `usesDataProtectionKeychain
	     = false` (the classic, non-protected keychain) would dodge the
	     access-group requirement is WRONG. secd rejects the call with the
	     exact same -34018 and the exact same message — "Client has neither
	     com.apple.application-identifier nor
	     com.apple.security.application-groups nor keychain-access-groups
	     entitlements" — with the flag true or false, AND with this sandbox
	     entitlement present or absent. On this macOS version, secd demands
	     that entitlement for ANY Keychain Services call from an ad-hoc
	     signed binary, sandboxed or not. There is no code-only fix; only a
	     real signing team unblocks the keychain in debug. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.network.server</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
